 a large money advantage, including $2.2 million in outside support", "Open seat removes incumbent risk but no polling exists to test it"], "rationale": "The district's R+11 lean combined with a Republican-friendly national environment points to a double-digit GOP margin, and every major rater places the race at Solid or Safe R. Alfonso's fundraising and outside-spending edge over Clark reinforces that picture, with no polling evidence suggesting a competitive race.", "watch": "Any late polling showing Clark within single digits, or a misstep by Alfonso such as the voting-age comments gaining traction, would be the main signal of a closer race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Tom Tiffany (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Tom Tiffany's retirement leaves this heavily Republican northwestern Wisconsin seat open, but the fundamentals still favor the GOP strongly. The district leans Republican by eleven points, and the national environment adds to that advantage. Michael Alfonso, who won a contested primary with family ties to a well-known predecessor, has out-raised Democrat Fred Clark and benefits from substantial outside spending. Clark, a former state legislator, has run an active campaign and pressed for debates, but Alfonso's refusal to appear at one has drawn only local attention.\n\nNo public general-election polling exists, so the outlook rests on district lean, the spending gap, and expert assessments. All nine raters call the race Solid or Safe for Republicans, and prediction markets give Democrats only a small chance. A Republican win by a wide margin is the clear expectation, with modest uncertainty from the lack of polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0554, "margin": -10.68}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0396, "margin": -13.0}]}